  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": "pdf-p12",
      "document_id": "G178",
      "pdf_page": 12,
      "page_markdown": "documents/G178.md#page-12",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "The approach to value analysis that is used in Business Architecture is derived from James Martin’s The Great Transition: Using the Seven Disciplines of Enterprise Engineering to Align People, Technology, and Strategy [Martin, 1995]. The value stream (a simple example of which is shown in Figure 1-1) is depicted as an end-to-end collection of value-adding activities that create an overall result for a customer, stakeholder, or end-user. In modeling terms, those value-adding activities are represented by value stream stages, each of which creates and adds incremental stakeholder value from one stage to the next.",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G178:p0012:b002",
            "G178:p0012:b003",
            "G178:p0012:b004",
            "G178:p0012:b005",
            "G178:p0012:b006",
            "G178:p0012:b007",
            "G178:p0012:b008"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Value streams may be externally triggered, such as a retail customer acquiring some merchandise, as shown in the example above. Value streams may also be internally triggered, such as a manager obtaining a new hire. Value streams may be defined at an enterprise level or at a business unit level. Either way, the complete set of value streams (as depicted in a value stream diagram or map) is simply the visual representation of all the value streams that denote an organization’s primary set of business activities. It is an aggregation of the multiple ways in which the enterprise creates value for its various stakeholders.",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G178:p0012:b010",
            "G178:p0012:b011",
            "G178:p0012:b012",
            "G178:p0012:b013",
            "G178:p0012:b014",
            "G178:p0012:b015",
            "G178:p0012:b016"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "selection": "same-page-context"
    },
    {
      "id": "pdf-p16",
      "document_id": "G178",
      "pdf_page": 16,
      "page_markdown": "documents/G178.md#page-16",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "Stakeholder value is rarely produced as the result of a single step or activity. More often, value is achieved through a series of sequential and/or parallel actions, or value stream stages, that incrementally create and add stakeholder value from one stage to the next.",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G178:p0016:b002",
            "G178:p0016:b003",
            "G178:p0016:b004"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "1. Name – Two to three words identifying what is (or will be) achieved by this stage",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G178:p0016:b006"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "selection": "same-page-context"
    },
    {
      "id": "pdf-p18",
      "document_id": "G178",
      "pdf_page": 18,
      "page_markdown": "documents/G178.md#page-18",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "We start with an external stakeholder value stream – the Acquire Retail Product value stream introduced in Figure 1-1 (shown below as Figure 4-1). A more comprehensive scenario that includes heat-mapping and decision-making processes uses the Recruit Employee value stream that was first introduced in the TOGAF® Series Guide: Business Capabilities.",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G178:p0018:b005",
            "G178:p0018:b006",
            "G178:p0018:b007",
            "G178:p0018:b008",
            "G178:p0018:b009"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The first step is to define the value stream (see Table 4-1), as discussed in Section 2.1:",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G178:p0018:b010"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "selection": "caption-reference-and-nearby-context"
    },
    {
      "id": "pdf-p19",
      "document_id": "G178",
      "pdf_page": 19,
      "page_markdown": "documents/G178.md#page-19",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "The second example focuses on creating internal business value via the activity of recruiting employees to join the business; see Table 4-3. It provides a more detailed example of mapping value stream stages to business capabilities and performing a gap analysis.",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G178:p0019:b046",
            "G178:p0019:b047",
            "G178:p0019:b048"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "How would a business determine whether this was an important value stream, worth the effort to model and apply to business decisions? It may result from a specific need or pain point that the business is currently suffering such as inefficiencies associated with disparate activities related to recruitment, or it may be part of a strategic assessment at an enterprise level to identify and document all of the core value streams for the company.",
          "source_block_ids": [
